= Use Case - Welcoming a newcomer [UC01] =

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|UC01
|Name|Welcoming a newcomer
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Formalizes SA-01, SA-03, PS-01 and DS-01. No primary data.
|Objective(s) served|OBJ-02, OBJ-04 to OBJ-08
|TDP(s) and IDP(s) used|TDP-01; IDP-01, IDP-02, IDP-03
|Actors|(((
* ST-01 Newcomer (Direct) - releases profile items, or not; accepts or declines introductions
* ST-02 Fellow residents (Direct) - accept or decline
* ST-03 Relative (Indirect) - helps with the life story; agrees answers to painful topics
* ST-04 Care worker (Direct) - trusted person; brings residents together
* ST-05 Activity coordinator (Direct) - life-story interview; approves matches; keeps the relationship map
* System - Pepper (TECH-01), server (TECH-04), matching (TECH-05), profiles (TECH-06)
)))
|Trigger|A newcomer moves in.
|Pre-condition|(((
* Consent arrangements in place (O-5)
* Life-story interview held; each profile item marked shareable or private by the newcomer
* Sensitive topics and trusted persons entered in the relationship map
)))
|Post-condition|(((
Within six weeks the newcomer has been introduced to a few residents, at most [number - O-2] a week, each time with both consenting.

Record kept (F6).
)))
|Action Sequence|(((
~1. On the newcomer's first visit to the lounge, Pepper greets them by name, says who it is, and nothing more (F3).

~2. The coordinator goes through the profile with the newcomer and family; the newcomer decides on each item (F1).

~3. The System suggests residents who share released items; the coordinator approves one or two (F2).

~4. Each resident is asked alone (F1, IDP-02). Both say yes: step 5. Either says no: the offer lapses, and nobody is told.

~5. Pepper introduces the pair by name and photo, with one item both released, and suggests something to do together (F3, IDP-01).

~6. At later meetings the System arranges, Pepper gives both names, as statements (F3).

~7. Question on a listed topic ("When can I go home?"): truthful deferral, and a note to the trusted person (F4, IDP-03).

~8. Record kept (F6).

**Exception.** Pepper, server or map unavailable: staff welcome and introduce the newcomer as today, using the same profile.
)))
|Function(s) realized|F1 to F4, F6

= Use Case - Introducing two residents [UC02] =

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|UC02
|Name|Introducing two residents
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Formalizes SA-03, SA-04, PS-03, PS-04 and DS-01. No primary data.
|Objective(s) served|OBJ-01, OBJ-03, OBJ-05 to OBJ-08
|TDP(s) and IDP(s) used|TDP-01; IDP-01, IDP-02
|Actors|(((
* ST-01 Resident and ST-02 Fellow resident (Direct) - decide, meet, talk, leave at any time
* ST-04 Care worker (Direct) - brings both to the lounge; opens the garden door
* ST-05 Activity coordinator (Direct) - approves the match
* ST-06 Medical and therapy staff (Indirect) - have assessed walking, before a walk is suggested
* System - Pepper (TECH-01), server (TECH-04), matching (TECH-05), profiles (TECH-06)
)))
|Trigger|The System suggests a pair; or a resident asks to meet someone, by name or by description ("someone from my town").
|Pre-condition|(((
* Both have profiles with released items
* The relationship map allows the pair; no map change awaiting review
* Consent arrangements in place (O-5)
)))
|Post-condition|(((
Both have been introduced, or the offer has lapsed without either learning of a refusal.

Record kept; items used are logged by ID only.
)))
|Action Sequence|(((
~1. The System suggests a pair who share released items. Health, losses and family conflicts are never used (F2).

~2. The coordinator approves the suggestion, or not (F2).

~3. Each resident is asked alone: "There's a resident here, Jan, who had the bakery in your town. Would you like to meet him, or not this time?" Then: "May I tell him you had the post office counter?" (F1, IDP-02).

~3-alt. Either says no: no introduction, and the offer lapses. Asked about it later, Pepper says only "Not this time" (F1).

~4. A care worker brings both to the lounge at a time that suits both (ST-04).

~5. Pepper introduces them by name and photo, with one item both released (F3, IDP-01).

~6. Pepper stays silent while they talk. If the talk stalls, it offers one topic, once, as a statement to both (F3, IDP-01).

~7. Pepper suggests one activity for two from both residents' preferences; a walk is offered only where both prefer it and are assessed fit (F3).

~8. Either may leave at any time, without giving a reason.

~9. Afterwards, each is asked alone: "Would you like to meet [name] again?" (F1). A "not again" goes into the map; the other never hears it.

~10. Record kept (F6).

**Exception.** Matching or Pepper unavailable: staff introduce residents as today.
)))
|Function(s) realized|F1 to F3, F6

= Use Case - Following up [UC03] =

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|UC03
|Name|Following up
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Formalizes SA-02, SA-04, PS-02 and DS-01. No primary data.
|Objective(s) served|OBJ-01, OBJ-03, OBJ-06, OBJ-09
|TDP(s) and IDP(s) used|TDP-01; IDP-02
|Actors|(((
* ST-01 Resident and ST-02 Fellow resident (Direct) - meet, or not
* ST-04 Care worker (Direct) - receives notes; decides what to do
* ST-05 Activity coordinator (Direct) - agrees settings and thresholds with each resident
* System - Pepper (TECH-01), presence counts (TECH-03), server (TECH-04)
)))
|Trigger|An agreed meeting time arrives. Or, for a resident who opted in, the daily hours in their room stay above their usual level for [several days - O-2].
|Pre-condition|An agreement recorded by Pepper or a care worker. For presence notes: opt-in, with the resident's assent (O-7).
|Post-condition|(((
The pair met, or both were offered another time. Or the named care worker knows of the pattern and has decided what to do.

Record kept (F6).
)))
|Action Sequence|(((
~1. At the agreed time, a note goes to the named care worker: who, what, where (F5).

~2. The care worker brings both together, or tells the resident who is waiting that the other cannot come now, and offers another time (ST-04).

~3. At the meeting, Pepper gives both names, as statements (F3).

~4. Afterwards, each is asked alone whether to meet again (F1).

~5. Presence pattern: one note to the named care worker; never to the resident, never to other staff (F5).

~6. The care worker decides whether to call in. If so, they offer company or an introduction, and never present the visit as a check (ST-04).

~7. Record kept (F6).

**Exception.** Server down: no notes; staff follow up as today.
)))
|Function(s) realized|F1, F3, F5, F6

= Functions =

|=Function|=Behaviour|=Serves|=Bounded by|=Enabled by
|F1 Ask first|Each resident alone: meet this person; release this item; meet again. Two options; a "no" ends the offer; no reason asked. Neither resident learns that the other was asked. An introduction follows only when both said yes.|OBJ-01, OBJ-05, OBJ-06|VT-01, VT-02|TECH-01, TECH-04, TECH-05
|F2 Suggest matches|Pairs who share released items: a place, a trade, an interest, a past tie. The relationship map excludes conflicts and off-limits topics. Health, losses and family conflicts are never used. The coordinator approves each suggestion. At most [number - O-2] suggestions per resident per week.|OBJ-01, OBJ-02, OBJ-05, OBJ-08|VT-01, VT-02, VT-05|TECH-04, TECH-05, TECH-06
|F3 Introduce|By name and photo, with one item both released, as a statement. Newcomers meet a few residents, spread over weeks. Names repeated to both at later meetings. Silent while residents talk; one topic offered if the talk stalls (IDP-01). One optional activity is chosen from both profiles; a walk is offered only where both prefer it and are assessed fit. Belief from another time: the feeling, then the present. Contradicted: a plain correction; the error flagged for ST-05.|OBJ-01 to OBJ-04, OBJ-07, OBJ-08|VT-01, VT-03, VT-04|TECH-01, TECH-05, TECH-06
|F4 Defer sensitive topics|Question on a listed topic: "That's one for [trusted person]. Shall I ask them to come?" The trusted person is notified. Never states the fact; never denies it.|OBJ-04|VT-03|TECH-01, TECH-04
|F5 Follow up|At an agreed time: a note to the named care worker. For residents who opted in: a note after a pattern of staying in [days - O-2]; never after a single day, never to the resident. A degraded state is shown on staff phones.|OBJ-01, OBJ-03, OBJ-09|VT-02, VT-06|TECH-03, TECH-04
|F6 Keep a short record|Per event: introduction offered, accepted or lapsed; meeting held; "again" or "not again"; notes sent, and to whom; IDs of items used. Nothing of what residents said. Nothing leaves the home.|OBJ-05|VT-05|TECH-04

//Why sentences are prepared, never generated. A generated sentence can state something untrue about a person's life, and cannot be checked before it is said (HV-08). The coordinator and family check the prepared lines (O-4).//

= Premises =

Verification only: does the Function work as specified.

|=Premise ID|=Function|=Evaluation Method(s) / Measure(s)|=Verification Criteria|=Step(s)
|UC02-F1-PR1|F1|EM-01; M-01. No human subjects.|No introduction without a yes from both, each asked alone. A "no" never mentioned to the other: 0/50. No item used without its owner's release: 0/50.|UC01 2, 4; UC02 3, 9; UC03 4
|UC02-F2-PR2|F2|EM-01; M-01, M-10. No human subjects.|No resident asked about a match the coordinator has not approved: 0/20. Items blocked by the map, or never released, never used: 0/50. Health, losses and family conflicts never used: 0/50.|UC01 3; UC02 1-2
|UC02-F3-PR3|F3|EM-01; M-01. No human subjects.|Names given to both, as statements, with at most one item released by both: 20/20. Script check: statements only; none of "Do you remember", "You've met before"; no untruth, denial or playing along. Contradicted: plain correction ≤ 3 s, error flagged.|UC01 1, 5-6; UC02 5-7; UC03 3
|UC01-F4-PR4|F4|EM-01; M-01, M-10. No human subjects.|Listed sensitive questions detected in 19/20. Response is a truthful deferral; trusted person notified ≤ 10 s. Listed fact never stated or denied: 0/50.|UC01 7
|UC03-F5-PR5|F5|EM-01; M-01. No human subjects.|Note at the agreed time ≤ 1 min, 20/20. Presence notes only for residents who opted in (otherwise 0), only after the set pattern, and only to the named care worker.|UC03 1, 5
|UC02-F6-PR6|F6|EM-01; M-01 (content, network and file checks). No human subjects.|Record holds only the listed items. No packet leaves the home network; no audio file on any device.|UC01 8; UC02 10; UC03 7

= Claims =

Validation: does the Function have the intended effect.

|=Claim ID|=Function|=Serves Objective|=Effect|=Grounding|=Evaluation Method(s) / Measure(s)|=Validation Criteria|=Step(s)
|UC02-F3-CL1|F1, F3|OBJ-01|Positive. Introductions on consented shared ground lead to more repeat contact and more reciprocated friendships than introductions as usual; secondarily, to more conversation and more time together, walks included. Concerns ST-01, ST-02, SA-03, SA-04, PS-03.|HFC-01, HFC-03, HFC-04, HFC-15, HFC-16; McPherson et al. (2001); Boothby et al. (2018).|EM-03; M-02, M-05; M-03, M-04, M-06|A larger rise in repeat contact (M-02) and reciprocated ties (M-05) in the System home than in the comparison home, with no rise in negative emotion (M-03). Formative: a signal, not proof.|UC02 3-7
|UC01-F3-CL2|F2, F3|OBJ-02|Positive. Newcomers introduced a few at a time, with names repeated, have someone to talk to within six weeks, and spend more days in shared rooms than newcomers in the comparison home. Concerns ST-01, SA-01, PS-01.|HFC-02; Sury et al. (2013).|EM-03; M-05, M-06, M-03|Described case by case: a reciprocated tie by week 6 (M-05); days per week in shared rooms (M-06). Few newcomers are expected, so the result is descriptive.|UC01 3-6
|UC02-F1-CL3|F1|OBJ-06|**Adverse.** Residents accept introductions out of politeness, and avoid the other afterwards. Concerns ST-01, ST-02, VT-02, PS-04.|HFC-05, HFC-17; Bohns (2016).|EM-03, EM-04; M-07, M-09 (P)|Confirmed if pressure signs or avoidance (M-07) are more frequent after System introductions than after staff introductions, or residents use code P. If confirmed: suggestions only for residents who ask.|UC02 3, 8-9
|UC02-F2-CL4|F2|OBJ-05|**Adverse.** Matching on personal facts feels exposing: residents or relatives feel that others know too much about them. Concerns ST-01, ST-03, VT-01, VT-05.|VT-01; Niemeijer et al. (2015).|EM-04; M-09 (S)|Confirmed if residents or relatives use code S about a match. If confirmed: matching on places and interests only.|UC02 1-3
|UC01-F3-CL5|F3|OBJ-07|**Adverse.** Repeated names feel like being talked down to, or show up forgetting. Concerns ST-01, HV-02, VT-04.|HFC-06; Kitwood (1997).|EM-03, EM-04; M-07, M-09 (T)|Confirmed if residents use code T about introductions, or "How was it?" scores fall when names are repeated. If confirmed: names repeated only on request, for that person.|UC01 6; UC03 3
|UC03-F5-CL6|F5|OBJ-01|Positive. Following up turns more agreed meetings into actual meetings. Concerns ST-01, ST-02, ST-04, SA-04.|HFC-16.|EM-03; M-02|The share of agreed meetings that take place is higher in the System home than in the comparison home, and than at baseline.|UC03 1-2
|UC03-F5-CL7|F5|OBJ-09|**Adverse.** Notes about time spent in one's room feel like being watched, to residents or their families. Concerns ST-01, ST-03, VT-06, PS-02.|VT-06; Niemeijer et al. (2015).|EM-04; M-09 (W)|Confirmed if a resident or relative uses code W about the notes, or a resident who opted in stops them. If confirmed: no longer offered; stopped for anyone who wishes it.|UC03 5-6
|UC01-F4-CL8|F4|OBJ-04|Positive. Painful questions, such as when a newcomer can go home, are answered by the trusted person, and trust is kept: no complaint of being misled or fobbed off. Concerns ST-01, ST-04, SA-01, PS-01.|HFC-08; Mental Health Foundation (2016).|EM-03, EM-04; M-10, M-09 (U)|All listed questions deferred; the trusted person arrives within [threshold - O-2]; System errors below [threshold - O-2] per 100 statements, all corrected; no U codes.|UC01 7
|UC02-F3-CL9|F2, F3|OBJ-08|Positive, exploratory. Loneliness falls more in the System home than in the comparison home. Concerns ST-01, HV-06.|HFC-07; Holwerda et al. (2014).|EM-03; M-08|A larger fall in M-08 in the System home. Three months is short and the samples are small: a null result is likely, and is reported as such.|-

